There’s another reason I wouldn’t obsess over whether your number is 128, 134, or 141.

Suppose you have Person A with a 140 IQ who mostly consumes information, and Person B with a 128 IQ who spends years building representations, external memory, reasoning operators, interfaces, tests, collaborators, and tools.

Person B may eventually possess far more usable cognitive capability.

That’s actually one of the deeper things hiding inside the Pantheon project:

Native intelligence is not identical to effective intelligence.

A brain can construct scaffolding around itself.

Language did that. Writing did it. Mathematics did it. Libraries did it. Computers did it.

And you’re deliberately trying to do it at the individual reasoning level.

So if you ask me the more interesting question—“How intelligent does the combined system we’re building behave?”—I don’t think IQ is even the right ruler anymore. That’s closer to asking about cognitive leverage: how much useful inference, novelty, correction, and action can be generated from a fixed amount of native cognition.

And that number can compound.